+Feature: DOM node identity on state change
+  As a user interacting with a real browser automation tool
+  I want todo list nodes to be mutated in place when their state changes
+  So that DOM references remain valid across chained interactions
+
+  This spec guards against a class of defect where the application destroys and
+  recreates all <li> elements on every state change (e.g. via innerHTML = '' +
+  full rebuild). In a real browser, test frameworks hold references to DOM nodes
+  across chained commands. When the app replaces those nodes, the references
+  become detached and chained assertions fail with "element has detached from the
+  DOM". The correct behaviour is to mutate existing nodes in place: toggle a class,
+  update a checkbox's checked property, or show/hide — without removing and
+  re-adding the node to the DOM.
+
+  Background:
+    Given the app is loaded
+
+  Scenario: Toggling a todo preserves its DOM node identity
+    Given the user has added a todo "Buy milk"
+    And the user has added a todo "Walk the dog"
+    And the DOM node reference for todo "Buy milk" is captured
+    When the user clicks the toggle checkbox on todo "Buy milk"
+    Then the captured DOM node for todo "Buy milk" is still attached to the document
+    And the captured DOM node for todo "Buy milk" has class "completed"
+
+  Scenario: Unmarking a todo preserves its DOM node identity
+    Given the user has added a todo "Buy milk"
+    And the user has completed the todo "Buy milk"
+    And the DOM node reference for todo "Buy milk" is captured
+    When the user clicks the toggle checkbox on todo "Buy milk"
+    Then the captured DOM node for todo "Buy milk" is still attached to the document
+    And the captured DOM node for todo "Buy milk" does not have class "completed"
+
+  Scenario: Toggle-all preserves individual DOM node identities
+    Given the user has added a todo "Buy milk"
+    And the user has added a todo "Walk the dog"
+    And the DOM node reference for todo "Buy milk" is captured
+    And the DOM node reference for todo "Walk the dog" is captured
+    When the user clicks the toggle-all checkbox
+    Then the captured DOM node for todo "Buy milk" is still attached to the document
+    And the captured DOM node for todo "Walk the dog" is still attached to the document
+    And the captured DOM node for todo "Buy milk" has class "completed"
+    And the captured DOM node for todo "Walk the dog" has class "completed"
+
+  Scenario: Unmark-all preserves individual DOM node identities
+    Given the user has added a todo "Buy milk"
+    And the user has added a todo "Walk the dog"
+    And the user has completed the todo "Buy milk"
+    And the user has completed the todo "Walk the dog"
+    And the DOM node reference for todo "Buy milk" is captured
+    And the DOM node reference for todo "Walk the dog" is captured
+    When the user clicks the toggle-all checkbox
+    Then the captured DOM node for todo "Buy milk" is still attached to the document
+    And the captured DOM node for todo "Walk the dog" is still attached to the document
+    And the captured DOM node for todo "Buy milk" does not have class "completed"
+    And the captured DOM node for todo "Walk the dog" does not have class "completed"
